--
-- junzip.exw
--
--  Does not support zip files larger than 1GB, and certainly not ZIP64.
--  Ditto multipart zips: a) floppies died decades ago, b) 7-Zip is free.
--  Ditto unexpected eof, .bz, .gz, .gz renamed as .zip, any header error,
--  incorrect comment length, trailing junk at the end of the file, wrong
--  crc, & in fact just about any other petty excuse it/you can think of.
--  Coped with 99% of the files I threw at it - but note there is nothing
--  difficult, unusual, or of any interest to me in finding one of the 1%
--  it rejects, that (eg) 7-Zip [somehow] manages to cope just fine with.
--  It should o/c support any undamaged file previously created by itself.
--  Conforms to ISO/IEC 21320-1:2015(E) - IT Document Container File, but
--  without support for ZIP64 (version 1 or any).
--
--  NOTE: Should 7-Zip show any kind of header error, crc failure, or exotic
--        compression method, I immediately deem that file as "unsupported".
--        Also, (eg) classes.zip from the archive has bit #8000 set in gpbf
--        without #0800, so meaningless and I'm not going anywhere near it.
--
-- This is not intended to be a replacement or rival to the likes of 7-Zip,
-- or to act as a suitable basis for any such thing, but to facilitate the 
-- creation of zip files that can be downloaded and unpacked automatically, 
-- and certainly not to even try coping with anything even slightly tricky.
--
-- Very inspiring, the page that convinced me to give all this a go:
--  https://codeandlife.com/2014/01/01/unzip-library-for-c/
--  https://codeandlife.com/2025/12/16/junzip-and-jzipview-updated/

-- The structure of a PKZip file (headers only, no block details):
--  When reading this note there can be anthing before the first local file
--  header, including a complete exe file, we're not supporting an archive
--  decryption header or archive extra data record, nor any Zip64 stuff or
--  digital signatures. Also, the os version is always/only 0/3/11.
-- https://users.cs.jmu.edu/buchhofp/forensics/formats/pkzip-printable.html
--
-- An Explanation of the Deflate Algorithm (no useful code):
--  https://zlib.net/feldspar.html
--
-- Obligatory:
--  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deflate
--  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ZIP_(file_format)
--
-- Very detailed, too much so, in all probability:
--  https://www.hanshq.net/zip.html
--
